# Revision Notes Feature Architecture

## Purpose

The Revision Notes feature is designed to help the user **retain knowledge systematically**, not simply store notes.

Every completed work session can produce a revision note describing what was learned during that session.

Unlike ordinary note-taking applications, revision notes in Omega are tightly coupled with work sessions, making them a chronological learning history.

The long-term objective is to evolve these notes into a complete revision system capable of tracking revision progress, scheduling future revisions, and helping the user remember important concepts.

---

# Design Philosophy

The feature follows four principles.

### 1. Work First

Revision notes are always attached to completed work.

```
Work Session
        ↓
Revision Note
```

A note never exists independently.

---

### 2. Sessions represent learning history

Each session records

* when work happened
* how long it lasted
* session title
* expected duration

A revision note records

* what was learned during that session

Therefore multiple sessions naturally create a learning timeline.

Example

```
Repository Pattern

↓

Session 1
Learned DAO

↓

Session 2
Implemented Repository

↓

Session 3
Added Flow support

↓

Session 4
Fixed bugs
```

No additional timeline table is required.

---

### 3. Attachments belong to notes indirectly

Attachments are attached to sessions.

```
Session

↓

Attachment
```

Since a session has one revision note,

attachments automatically belong to that note.

This avoids another level of indirection.

---

### 4. Separation of Responsibilities

The feature separates

Knowledge

from

Revision Progress

Knowledge is permanent.

Revision progress changes continuously.

These responsibilities must remain independent.

